                id: "stone-watchful-passage", name: "Waiting for the stone to pass (with or without tamsulosin)", tier: .conservative,
                summary: "Pain relief, fluids and time, with a follow-up scan, for a stone that is likely to pass on its own. Some doctors add tamsulosin, a medicine that relaxes the ureter.",
                whatHappens: ["Anti-inflammatory pain relievers such as ibuprofen or ketorolac, which work at least as well as opioids for stone pain.", "Sometimes tamsulosin once a day for up to 4 weeks.", "Straining your urine to catch the stone so it can be tested.", "A follow-up X-ray, ultrasound or CT to check it has passed."],
                anesthesia: "None", hospitalStay: "None", recovery: "Most stones that pass do so within 2–4 weeks.",
                bestFor: ["Stones under about 10 mm with pain you can manage", "No signs of infection and normal kidney function"],
                lessSuitableIf: ["Fever or signs of infection with a blocked kidney", "A single kidney or both kidneys blocked", "Large stones (over about 10 mm)", "Pregnancy (needs specialist advice)"],
                outcomes: OptionOutcomes(improvement: PercentRange(50, 80), seriousComplications: PercentRange(1, 3), furtherTreatment: PercentRange(15, 40)),
                keyFindings: [
                    "In the SUSPEND trial of 1,167 people, about 8 in 10 stones passed within 4 weeks whether people took tamsulosin, nifedipine or a placebo (Pickard 2015).",
                    "In the STONE trial of 512 emergency department patients with stones under 9 mm, tamsulosin did not increase passage at 4 weeks (about 50% vs 47%) (Meltzer 2018).",
                    "Pooled trials suggest tamsulosin may help stones of about 5–10 mm low in the ureter (Ye 2018; Hollingsworth 2016).",
                ],
                sideEffects: [
                    SE.make("More episodes of severe pain while waiting", "Stone pain can come and go until it passes.", 30, 60, .moderate),
                    SE.make("Return visits to the emergency department", "Usually for pain.", 10, 25, .moderate),
                    SE.make("Dizziness or little semen when climaxing (tamsulosin)", "Mild and stops when the medicine is stopped.", 5, 15, .mild),
                    SE.make("Infection behind a blocked stone", "Needs urgent drainage. Know the warning signs: fever and chills.", 1, 3, .serious),
                    SE.nsaidStomach,
