                evidence: .strong,
                availability: "Usually started in the emergency department or by a primary care doctor. Tamsulosin is a low-cost generic medicine; using it for stones is common but off-label.",
                sources: ["Pickard R et al. Lancet 2015;386:341–9", "Meltzer AC et al. JAMA Intern Med 2018;178:1051–7", "Ye Z et al. Eur Urol 2018;73:385–91", "Hollingsworth JM et al. BMJ 2016;355:i6112"]),

            TreatmentOption(
                id: "stone-prevention", name: "Preventing new stones (fluids, diet and medicines)", tier: .conservative,
                summary: "Drinking enough to make about 2.5 quarts (2.5 liters) of urine a day, adjusting diet, and sometimes medicines, based on stone and urine tests. This doesn't remove a stone you have now but lowers the chance of the next one.",
                whatHappens: ["Testing the stone and often a 24-hour urine collection.", "Drinking more water through the day; urine should look pale.", "Less salt and animal protein, enough calcium from food, and fewer sugary drinks.", "Sometimes medicines such as potassium citrate, a thiazide water pill or allopurinol, depending on your tests."],
                anesthesia: "None", hospitalStay: "None", recovery: "Ongoing habits.",
